      "id": 1,
      "text": "Prairie Grove was first settled by white people around 1829 by hunter Thomas Wagnon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://washcohistoricalsociety.org/prairie_grove",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first post office, Sweet Home, operated from 1840 to 1846 before a second office named Ada opened in 1857.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://washcohistoricalsociety.org/prairie_grove",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Prairie Grove’s canning industry kept jobs alive during the Great Depression, with two factories still running through World War II.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://washcohistoricalsociety.org/prairie_grove",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The railroad originally planned Prairie Grove, and the later gas boom drew factories to the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://hometownhistorymaps.com/pages/history-of-prairie-grove-arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Historic North Mock Street still hosts some of the town’s oldest buildings, including the Southern Mercantile Building.",
